window.docContents[123].resolve({"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.CryptoGames.AEAD.CBCDefs/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___CryptoGames___AEAD___CBCDefs":{"contents":"Paper › LibsignalBinary/Specifications/CryptoGames/AEAD › CBCDefs.leanTier 2 · definitions\n\nTier 2 · definitions used by the computational proof and symbolic proof 6 headline theorems depend on it 54 of 150 lines trusted trust banner B3, trusted in part source ↗Read the definitions shown. Only the declarations that the headline statements use are shown, with the namespace, section, open and variable commands needed to read them. The collapsed runs are theorems, which Lean checks, and definitions that no headline statement uses.\n\nDeclarations on this page (11)abbrev Blockabbrev AesKeydef cbcEncryptabbrev Plaindef nBlocksdef padLendef pkcs7Bytesdef bytesToBlockdef pkcs7Blocksdef cbcSchemeabbrev cbcPkcs7EncryptOnly\n\nAES-256-CBC with PKCS#7 padding as a one-time cipherTrusted in part. The theorems of Computational/Theorems/ use only AesKey, Block, Plain, bytesToBlock, cbcEncrypt, cbcScheme, nBlocks, padLen, pkcs7Blocks, pkcs7Bytes, cbcPkcs7EncryptOnly from this file: read them. Everything else here is checked by Lean. See Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md.Models libsignal's signal_crypto::aes_256_cbc_encrypt over an abstract AES-256 block function E : AesKey → Block → Block (DEV-AEAD14), together with monadic CBC cbcM, the PRF wrapper blockPRF, and the one-time IND-CPA-to-PRF reduction cbcRed (CBC.lean). Decryption is specified separately in CBCDecDefs.lean.\n\n\n\nabbrev BlockL35–36 ↗#A 128-bit AES block.\n\nabbrev AesKeyL38–39 ↗#A 256-bit AES key.\n\ndef cbcEncryptL41–52 ↗#CBC encryption of the blocks P with block function f, starting from the chaining value prev (the IV): C₁ = f (P₁ ⊕ prev), Cᵢ₊₁ = f (Pᵢ₊₁ ⊕ Cᵢ).DEV-AEAD8 (libsignal): the IV is part of the key. libsignal derives the IV with HKDF (the HMAC-based key derivation) together with the keys, and never transmits it. So the IV is part of the one-time key (k, iv) and not of the ciphertext. Effect: none.\n\n1 declaration not shown (lines 54–62): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoGames.CBC.cbcM\n\nPKCS#7 padding\n\nabbrev PlainL66–73 ↗#A plaintext of exactly ℓ bytes. CBC with PKCS#7 reveals the length, so the game fixes it.DEV-AEAD2 (libsignal): one plaintext length per session. ACD19 Def. 2 compares the real ciphertext with a uniformly random one. AES-CBC with PKCS#7 padding reveals the number of blocks, so every plaintext of a session has the same length ℓ; the end-to-end adversary's messages have type CBC.Plain ℓ. Effect: restricts the adversary (no claim for sessions that mix message lengths).\n\ndef nBlocksL75–76 ↗#Number of ciphertext blocks for an ℓ-byte plaintext.\n\ndef padLenL78–79 ↗#PKCS#7 pad length: 16 − (ℓ mod 16), always in 1..16.\n\ndef pkcs7BytesL81–83 ↗#The padded byte string: m followed by padLen ℓ copies of the byte padLen ℓ.\n\ndef bytesToBlockL85–88 ↗#Little-endian block of (up to) 16 bytes: byte j occupies bits 8j .. 8j+7, as in an AArch64 16-byte load.\n\ndef pkcs7BlocksL90–92 ↗#PKCS#7 padding split into nBlocks ℓ blocks: block i is bytes 16i .. 16i+15.\n\nThe cipher and the PRF\n\ndef cbcSchemeL96–102 ↗#CBC with key (k, iv) applied to a deterministic block encoding encode of the message. Decryption is out of scope and always rejects.\n\nabbrev cbcPkcs7EncryptOnlyL104–112 ↗#libsignal's AES-256-CBC with PKCS#7 padding on ℓ-byte plaintexts, AES abstracted as E.DEV-AEAD14 (machine model): AES and SHA-256 are arbitrary functions. The AES-256 block function E here and the SHA-256 compression function F of AEAD/HMACDefs.lean are parameters, because in the binary they are hooks whose machine code is not verified (DEV-E8). The assumptions (blockPRF E and HMAC are PRFs) are about whatever the hooks compute. Effect: none.\n\n3 declarations not shown (lines 114–147): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoGames.CBC.blockPRF, CryptoGames.CBC.cbcRedImpl, CryptoGames.CBC.cbcRed\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"LibsignalBinary/Specifications/CryptoGames/AEAD/CBCDefs.lean","id":"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.CryptoGames.AEAD.CBCDefs/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___CryptoGames___AEAD___CBCDefs"},"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.LibsignalHmac/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___LibsignalHmac":{"contents":"Paper › LibsignalBinary/Specifications › LibsignalHmac.leanTier 2 · definitions\n\nTier 2 · definitions used by the symbolic proof 2 headline theorems depend on it 13 of 52 lines trusted trust banner B2, trusted: definitions used by the statement source ↗Read the definitions shown. Only the declarations that the headline statements use are shown, with the namespace, section, open and variable commands needed to read them. The collapsed runs are theorems, which Lean checks, and definitions that no headline statement uses.\n\nSHA-256 Compression Hook and Chaining-State LayoutTrusted: definitions used by the statement. Memory layout of sha2's 8-word little-endian SHA-256 chaining state (shaStateBytes, readShaState) and the extern hook compress256Hook modeling sha2::sha256::compress256(state: &mut [u32; 8], blocks: &[[u8; 64]]) over an abstract compression function F : ShaState → List (BitVec 8) → ShaState. See Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md.\n\n\n\ndef shaStateBytesL35–37 ↗#The 32 bytes of a chaining state as stored by sha2 ([u32; 8], native LE words).\n\ndef readShaStateL39–41 ↗#The chaining state stored at a: 8 LE 32-bit words.\n\ndef compress256HookL43–49 ↗#sha2::sha256::compress256(state = x0, blocks.ptr = x1, blocks.len = x2): with st the 8 LE words at x0 and bs the x2 · 64 bytes at x1, mem[x0 .. x0+32] := shaStateBytes ((chunks64 bs).foldl F st) (all inputs read before writing); pc := lr.\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"LibsignalBinary/Specifications/LibsignalHmac.lean","id":"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.LibsignalHmac/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___LibsignalHmac"},"/Tier-3___-trusted___-not-read/AArch64-semantics/Arm64.Trusted.Spec.RustRuntime/#file-Arm64___Trusted___Spec___RustRuntime":{"contents":"Paper › arm64-semantics/Arm64/Trusted/Spec › RustRuntime.leanTier 3 · arm64-semantics\n\nTier 3 · arm64-semantics used by the symbolic proof 3 headline theorems depend on it 18 of 51 lines trusted source ↗Trusted, reviewed with the engine. The AArch64 model in arm64-semantics is Tier 3: it is reviewed in its own repository (check-trust-guards). The declarations that the headline statements use are shown for completeness.\n\nRust Runtime Panic HooksModels Rust panic = abort entry points by wrapping a Program hook table (Rust.withPanics) so calls to core::panicking::*, unwrap/expect failure paths, slice bounds checks, and allocation error handlers transition s.status to .Panicked.\n\n\n\ndef isPanicSymbolL26–40 ↗#Demangled names of functions that start a panic. Everything in core::panicking panics; the others are the out-of-line panic paths of unwrap/expect, slice indexing, and allocation failure.\n\ndef panickedL42–44 ↗#The effect of entering a panic.\n\n1 declaration not shown (lines 46–49): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesArm64.Rust.withPanics\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"arm64-semantics/Arm64/Trusted/Spec/RustRuntime.lean","id":"/Tier-3___-trusted___-not-read/AArch64-semantics/Arm64.Trusted.Spec.RustRuntime/#file-Arm64___Trusted___Spec___RustRuntime"}});